WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.860
[Graph indicating Deaths from Various Cancers - U.S.A.]

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.540
[Narrator:] Soon after 1930, the deaths from lung cancer turned sharply upwards. 

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:40.740
By 1980, if the present rate continues, there will be 90,000 deaths per year. Or will there?

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:50.530
Today, physicians are increasingly the doing the things that can reduce this mortality and make a different story possible.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:55.080
[X-ray films of a person's lungs are placed on a viewer.]

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:16.430
[G. Hugh Lawrence, M.D., Surgeon:] By pre-operative selection of potentially favorable lesions,

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:24.870
we have experienced a fourfold increase in the overall survival from bronchogenic carcinoma.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:32.860
[Oscar Auerbach, M.D., Pathologist:] There is no question in my mind that precancerous lesions from smoking are reversible.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:41.090
[Thomas Carlile, M.D., Radiologist:] Today it is increasingly important for all physicians to have an aggressive attitude about this disease

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.370
because the cure rates can be further improved.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:53.810
[Dr. Lawrence:] Early thoracotomy is indicated in all new lesions which defy the usual methods of diagnosis.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:59.770
These small asymptomatic tumors have the best prognosis.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:07.010
[Dr. Auerbach:] Here is the photograph of a normal lung in a non-smoker. 

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:10.980
And here is the photograph of a lung cancer in a man who had smoked most of his adult life.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.260
[Side-by-side photos show a healthy lung labeled Non-Smoker and a cancerous one labeled Cancer in Heavy Smoker.]

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:27.160
After a study of some 1,972 autopsies at our hospital, we are able to determine when the man has smoked heavily over a long period of time.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:37.250
We are also able to determine when a heavy cigarette smoker has given up the habit for a considerable period of time prior to his death.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.020
[Image of bronchial epithelium is shown with arrows pointing to different elements.]

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:43.200
Normal bronchial epithelium consists of a layer of ciliated columnar cells.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:52.350
Beneath it is a layer of one or two basal cells of uniform size with small evenly staining nuclei. 

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:57.290
The basal cells lie on a basement membrane.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:02.670
Early changes toward malignancy can be recognized where there is a conspicuous

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:10.950
and abnormal increase in the number of basal cells with numerous atypical nuclei.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:19.680
Carcinoma in-situ is characterized by the fact that there's an easily identified increase of the basal cells,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:28.760
resulting in a thickening of the epithelium of five or more rows, and the absence of cilium. 

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.360
There is as yet no invasion of the basement membrane. 

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:39.920
All of the cells are atypical with prominent hyperchromatic nuclei.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.940
With an early invasive bronchogenic carcinoma,

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:52.150
there is an extensive cellular atypism and extension beyond the basement membrane.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:58.560
Even at this late stage of development, the lesion is not large enough to be seen in the x-ray

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.550
nor is it large enough to produce any clinical symptoms.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:09.830
Now let us see how the process reverses itself when the individual has given up smoking.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.320
[Image of lung cells.]

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:19.970
Here's an example of an individual who had smoked two packs of cigarettes a day for twenty years and had given up the habit for five.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:21.590
[Graph titled "% of Tissue Slides with Atypical Nuclei"]

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:28.450
In a recent study, we found that cells with atypical nuclei were present in only 1.2 percent of the slides

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:33.060
from those who had never smoked regularly, compared with 93 percent

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:38.670
among slides from those who were smokers up to the time of their last illness.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:44.410
Among those who had smoked for at least ten years but not within five years prior to death,

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:50.640
there were only six percent of the slides with cells with atypical nuclei.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:05:00.690
Perhaps the greatest motivation for patients to give up the habit of smoking lies in the fact that the process is reversible.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:06.500
[Dr. Carlile:] Certainly, it is the responsibility of physicians to discourage their patients from smoking.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:14.820
However, the more immediate problem is the management of patients who have lung cancer or are in the high-risk group.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:19.160
Periodic chest x-ray is the most widely available and simplest method for the detection of lung cancer.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.100
[A man is shown getting an x-ray, then medical staff are seen standing in front of shelves and charts and films.]

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.060
Routine films on asymptomatic patients, particularly on smokers,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:35.850
besides disclosing unsuspected lesions, serve as a valuable purpose for future baseline of comparisons.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:42.590
[The doctor places x-ray films on the viewer and flips on the light.]

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:51.880
Is the early diagnosis of lung cancer really worthwhile when the national average is less than ten percent in overall survival?

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.700
The answer very definitely is yes.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:02.340
I would like to illustrate several points that are very important in the management of lung cancer with some chest x-rays.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:09.690
This first patient represents an early diagnosis and represents the value of the baseline film. 

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:14.100
This film of several years ago was normal.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.090
We then have a film that was taken about two years later, and this was also called normal.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:20.180
[The doctor describes what he seeing on various x-ray films.]

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:26.790
However, because this was a heavy smoker, and because of suspicion of pulmonary pathology,

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.260
an additional film was taken six months later,

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:38.790
and at that time, for the first time, a small density, less than one cm in diameter, was found hidden in part by this rib.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.560
[X-ray film is shown with arrow pointing at lesion.]

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:48.050
As we looked back at the film of six months ago, we could see the genesis of this lesion.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:53.830
This patient had a conventional diagnostic workup, and following that a right upper lobectomy,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:07:01.010
and we see here the rib resection and the result of lobectomy and the expansion of the remaining lung

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:04.710
to fill up the space of the lobe that was removed.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:08.240
This patient lived more than five years.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:15.830
I would like to be able to say that early diagnosis of this nature is the complete answer to lung cancer.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:22.430
It does improve curability, but there are some lesions that are so biologically unfavorable

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:35.300
that in spite of everything we do, we still cannot obtain survival for the patient.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:40.120
This next series of films, for example, illustrates that point.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:43.250
[The doctor places a new set of films on the viewer.]

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:52.090
First we have a chest x-ray with a rather subtle lesion above the right hilar root and again, in part, behind the rib.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:59.140
However, this was recognized, and thought to be suspicious enough to warrant further investigation.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:06.440
A bronchogram was done, demonstrating obstruction of the bronchial going to the particular area in suspicion.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:11.210
Then finally the lesion was found to be inoperable,

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:20.450
and the patient was dead of his disease within a matter of three or four months, in spite of the fact that this was a very small lesion.

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:25.560
While we have been stressing the importance of lesions of small size, 

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.400
this case illustrates another point, and that is the location of the tumor.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.380
[Animated image of lungs.]

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.010
When they are placed centrally and close to the mediastinum,

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:41.190
they rapidly metastasize to the mediastinal nodes and become inoperable.

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:46.800
Thus, the patient is prevented an opportunity of a cure even when the lesion is small.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:53.000
This again illustrates the inherent biological potential of certain types of lung cancer,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.100
which make it such an unfavorable disease in contrast to other forms of cancer.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.330
[Graph indicating Deaths from Various Cancers--U.S.A.]

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:04.330
It also emphasizes the necessity of prevention.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:08.160
There is another type of situation for which we must be alert.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:13.260
This is the time when pneumonia masks a lung cancer and may prevent its diagnosis.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:23.610
[The doctor places x-ray films on the viewer.]

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:33.090
This lady had recurrent attacks of bronchitis over a period of years, and they responded to antibiotics and customary therapy.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:39.150
However, on one occasion, a lesion appeared in the region of the lingula of her left upper lobe,

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:42.010
and this persisted more than two weeks.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:48.900
Under antibiotic therapy, it not only didn't go away, but its margins became more distinct and sharper,

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:53.510
and this led to the suspicion that it might be a carcinoma.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:56.470
Further studies were made, as is our custom,

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:04.170
and finally the patient was submitted to exploratory thoracotomy and lobectomy.

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:10.540
This proved to be an alveolar cell carcinoma of relatively small size without lymph node metastasis,

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:15.380
and she is alive and well many years later. 

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.520
In addition to the problem of pneumonia,

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:24.130
there are other diseases that may cover lung cancer and obscure it.

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:27.700
[A new set of x-ray films is mounted on the viewer.]

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:32.530
This particular patient had a cavity in his right upper lobe.

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:39.020
It doesn't look particularly like tuberculosis, yet this must be considered in the differential diagnosis.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:44.490
However, the right hilar root is rather heavy, and there is some lower lobe pulmonary fibrosis.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:49.290
This patient was explored and proved to have an inoperable lung cancer. 

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:54.930
Any lung shadow that persists more than two weeks warrants suspicion of cancer,

00:10:54.930 --> 00:11:00.240
and further diagnostic procedures should be undertaken at once.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.400
Among other x-ray diagnostic tools is fluoroscopy, which allows one to observe the pulmonary dynamics.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:08.600
[A man is shown in a medical office receiving a fluoroscopy, then sitting down while the doctor examines him.]

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:13.790
Bronchography may outline a small obstruction. 

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:18.010
Tomography, that is body section roentgenography, may reveal additional details.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.890
[A man is shown lying down with a tomography machine operating above him.]

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:28.220
[Dr. Lawrence:] Early exploratory thoracotomy is indicated whenever the diagnosis is in question,

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:35.410
or when endoscopic evaluation indicates that a curative resection is feasible.

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:43.880
Before a thoracotomy, each patient should be carefully evaluated in order to delineate the nature of the lesion

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:50.190
and its extent as well as the patient's cardiopulmonary reserve.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:57.360
Sputum cytology is an integral part of this diagnostic evaluation.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.300
In the poor-risk patient, it may be the only means of safely establishing the diagnosis.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:05.340
[A nurse places an oxygen mask on patient, then removes it, at which time he coughs into a cup.]

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:11.940
When positive, it not only reinforces the diagnostic suspicion and the indication for exploration,

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:14.140
it allows the surgeon to proceed with the definitive resection

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.180
[A sample is examined through a microscope.]

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:23.220
at the time of thoracotomy without risking spread of the lesion by operative biopsy.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:26.050
Bronchoscopy is another important diagnostic procedure.

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:29.060
[A patient lying down and swathed in surgical gowns undergoes a bronchoscopy, in which a long, thin surgical instrument is inserted into the throat.]

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:37.810
It may visualize a centrally-located lesion.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:42.900
A right-angle telescope increases the extent of this observation.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:46.530
[A view of tissue through a telescope is shown.]

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:53.500
Bronchoscopy may provide diagnostic tissue or cytologic washings.

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:58.660
Findings of bronchoscopy help define resectability.

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:05.150
We have almost routinely performed scalene lymph node biopsy at the time of bronchoscopy.

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:08.800
It has yielded positive nodes in one quarter of our patients.

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:11.920
[Tissue is removed with a scalpel and smeared on a sterile cloth.]

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:15.700
These are, in our opinion, criteria of inoperability.

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:21.350
Mediastinoscopy, an extension of node sampling to within the mediastinum

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:28.650
may be helpful in establishing a diagnosis and in determining operability.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:31.600
The scope is passed into the mediastinum through a cervical incision,

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.490
[The procedure is demonstrated.] 

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:42.510
and nodes along the trachea, carina, and main stem bronchi are visualized and biopsied as indicated.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:46.460
Involvement of the laryngeal nerve, superior vena cava,

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:48.500
[Animated diagram points out these parts of the body.]

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:55.550
or distant metastases are other criteria which usually indicate inoperability. 

00:13:55.550 --> 00:14:01.200
However, superior sulcus tumors, invading the apex of the thorax,

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:08.590
have on occasion responded favorably to combined resection and radiation therapy.

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:14.520
Exploratory thoracotomy is performed in the absence of the above-mentioned criteria of inoperability,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.140
[Surgeons are gathered around a patient on the operating table.]

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:23.500
and resection is offered to all patients without mediastinal spread or significant chest wall invasion.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:28.940
It is unquestionably the surest way of arriving at a definitive diagnosis

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:35.720
and hopefully effecting cure by proceeding with the definitive resection.

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:41.410
Lobectomy is the operation of choice in bronchogenic carcinoma when the tumor is confined to the lobe.

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:46.000
[Animated diagram of the lungs shows a lesion on the right lung.]

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:56.660
Pneumenectomy is performed when the tumor crosses the fissure or when the associated interlobe bar or hilar lymph nodes are involved.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:09.910
Pneumenectomy is usually performed intrapericardially in order to obtain a wider vascular margin with an end block resection of involved lymph nodes.

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:18.090
Extended resection of mediastinal nodes, chest wall, and contiguous vascular structures is reserved

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:26.200
for the younger patients who are better able to tolerate the increased morbidity of the procedure.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:31.160
The factor which has the greatest influence upon survival is the size of the lesion.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.700
[The lung lobe is removed from the patient on the operating table and the tumor examined.]

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:44.550
Our experience indicates the favorable nature of lesions of three centimeters or less confined to one lobe.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:48.430
[Dr. Carlile:] What can we offer to the patient whose lung cancer is inoperable

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:52.550
or in whom surgery cannot remove all of the disease?

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:55.370
Radiation therapy is a valuable adjunct in the management of lung cancer.

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:57.820
[A man lying on a gurney is positioned under a machine that will irradiate his lung tumor.]

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:02.710
In fact, a cure can be effected in a small but definite number of patients.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:08.910
There are some situations where its intelligent and judicious use can control certain symptoms

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:14.770
such as hemorrhage, infection behind obstruction, and particularly of pain.

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:21.110
There are some special situations, such as superior sulcus tumors, superior vena caval syndrome,

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:27.160
and pleural effusions where effective control can be obtained by radiation.

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:32.040
Pre-operative irradiation is being evaluated today in a number of institutions, 

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:35.180
and the final answer on it is not yet in.

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:39.460
Chemotherapy plays a limited role in the management of lung cancer 

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:43.940
in contrast to other forms of disseminated cancer.

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:49.820
However, there are some special situations where it may be of value.

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:55.590
The surgical curability of lung cancer was demonstrated more than thirty years ago.

00:16:55.590 --> 00:17:03.030
Since that time, we have learned much about its prevention and also about its diagnosis and treatment.

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:11.150
The fate of the individual patient frequently depends upon the prompt and full application of this knowledge.

00:18:03.070 --> 00:23:22.358
[Screen goes dark]

